Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>Appendix A - JavaScript quick reference</H1>
<HR>
This appendix is a quick reminder of the JavaScript the book uses, grouped by subject, with a short example of
each. The chapter that explains each idea is given in brackets; a few useful things the book doesn't use are
marked <I>(not used in this book)</I>.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Comments and statements</H2>
<PRE>
// a comment, to the end of the line                        (1)
/* a comment that can
   run over several lines */
print('Hello');           // a statement ends with ;
</PRE>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Values</H2>
<PRE>
42   3.14   -7   1e6          numbers                      (2)
'text'   "text"               strings                      (1)
`Total: ${a + b}`             template string              (2)
true   false                  booleans                     (3)
undefined                     "no value yet"               (8)
null                          "deliberately nothing"       (8)
NaN                           "not a number", Number('x')  (2)
[1, 2, 3]                     an array                     (5)
{ name: 'Ann', age: 12 }      an object                    (5)
</PRE>
<CODE>typeof x</CODE> gives the type of a value as a string: <CODE>'number'</CODE>, <CODE>'string'</CODE>,
<CODE>'boolean'</CODE>, <CODE>'undefined'</CODE>, <CODE>'object'</CODE> or <CODE>'function'</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Variables</H2>
<PRE>
const width = 400;       // can't be given a new value      (2)
let score = 0;           // can
score = score + 1;
score++;  score--;       // add or take away 1              (3)
score += 10;  score -= 2;  // short forms (not used in this book)
</PRE>
Names may contain letters, digits, <CODE>_</CODE> and <CODE>$</CODE>, but can't start with a digit. Capitals
matter: <CODE>score</CODE> and <CODE>Score</CODE> are different variables.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Operators</H2>
<PRE>
+  -  *  /           arithmetic                             (2)
%                    remainder: 7 % 3 is 1
**                   power: 2 ** 10 is 1024
+                    also joins strings: 'a' + 'b' is 'ab'
===  !==             equal, not equal                       (3)
&lt;  &lt;=  &gt;  &gt;=         less than, greater than ...
&amp;&amp;                   and: both are true
||                   or: either is true
!                    not
a ? b : c            b if a is true, else c (not used in this book)
</PRE>
Use <CODE>===</CODE> and <CODE>!==</CODE>, not <CODE>==</CODE> and <CODE>!=</CODE>, which try to convert
values before comparing them and sometimes give surprising answers.<P>
<CODE>false</CODE>, <CODE>0</CODE>, <CODE>''</CODE>, <CODE>null</CODE>, <CODE>undefined</CODE> and
<CODE>NaN</CODE> count as false in an <CODE>if</CODE>; everything else counts as true (chapter 8). <CODE>a || b</CODE> gives <CODE>a</CODE> if it
counts as true, otherwise <CODE>b</CODE>, which makes it handy for defaults: <CODE>Number(text) || 0</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Decisions</H2>
<PRE>
if (age &lt; 13) {                                             (3)
  print('child');
} else if (age &lt; 20) {
  print('teenager');
} else {
  print('adult');
}
</PRE>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Loops</H2>
<PRE>
for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++) { ... }       count 1 to 10    (3)
while (lives &gt; 0) { ... }                   while true
do { ... } while (again);                   at least once    (13)
for (const item of list) { ... }            each array item  (5)
break;                                      leave the loop
continue;                                   next time round
</PRE>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Functions</H2>
<PRE>
function area(w, h) {                                        (4)
  return w * h;
}
const double = (x) =&gt; x * 2;          arrow function         (4)
const add = (a, b) =&gt; {                with a body
  return a + b;
};
async function load() { ... await ... }   can use await
</PRE>
A variable declared inside a function (or any <CODE>{ }</CODE> block) exists only inside it. A function
declared inside another can use the outer one's variables. Functions are values: they can be kept in
variables, stored in objects and passed to other functions, as event handlers are.<P>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Strings</H2>
<PRE>
s.length                    number of characters             (1)
s[0]                        first character
s.toUpperCase()   s.toLowerCase()
s.trim()                    without spaces at the ends
s.includes('x')             true if 'x' is in s
s.indexOf('x')              where 'x' is, or -1
s.slice(2, 5)               characters 2 to 4
s.split(' ')                an array of the words            (5)
s.padStart(6)   s.padEnd(10)   pad with spaces to a width   (5)
s.repeat(3)                 s three times
String(42)                  '42'
Number('42')                42  (NaN if it isn't a number)    (2)
</PRE>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>Numbers and Math</H2>
<PRE>
n.toFixed(2)                '3.14': 2 decimal places         (2)
Math.round(n)   Math.floor(n)   Math.ceil(n)
Math.abs(n)   Math.sqrt(n)   Math.min(a, b)   Math.max(a, b)
Math.PI   Math.sin(a)   Math.cos(a)     angles in radians    (7)
Math.random()               0 up to (not including) 1        (3)
Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1       a dice throw, 1 to 6
</PRE>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Arrays</H2>
<PRE>
const a = [3, 1, 2];                                          (5)
a.length            3
a[0]                3 (the first item)
a.push(4)           add at the end
a.pop()             remove from the end, and give it back
a.unshift(0)        add at the start                          (13)
a.shift()           remove from the start
a.includes(2)       true
a.indexOf(2)        where 2 is, or -1
a.join(', ')        '3, 1, 2'
a.slice(1, 3)       a new array of items 1 and 2
a.sort((x, y) =&gt; x - y)       sort numbers into order       (5)
a.filter((x) =&gt; x &gt; 1)         [3, 2]: the items that pass  (12)
a.map((x) =&gt; x * 2)            [6, 2, 4]: a new array
a.forEach((x) =&gt; print(x))     do something with each
</PRE>
<CODE>push</CODE>, <CODE>sort</CODE> and the rest are <I>methods</I>: functions that belong to a value and
are called with a dot (chapter 5). <CODE>shift</CODE>, <CODE>slice</CODE>, <CODE>map</CODE> and
<CODE>forEach</CODE> are not used in this book.<P>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Objects</H2>
<PRE>
const p = { name: 'Ann', score: 12 };                        (5)
p.name              'Ann'
p['name']           the same
p[key]              the property whose name is in key         (13)
p.score = 13;       change a property
p.level = 2;        add one
Object.keys(p)      ['name', 'score', 'level'] (not used in this book)
</PRE>

<A NAME=S12></A>
<H2>Modules</H2>
<PRE>
export function drawBoard(g) { ... }      in Board           (11)
export const CELL = 16;
import { drawBoard, CELL } from './Board';
import { print, Menu } from 'riscos';
export default function start(task, ctx) { ... }
</PRE>
A file that uses <CODE>import</CODE> or <CODE>export</CODE> is a module, and runs by calling its
<CODE>start</CODE> function. See appendix B for how imports are found.<P>

<A NAME=S13></A>
<H2>Waiting: async and await</H2>
<PRE>
const name = await input('Name? ');                           (1)
await sleep(1000);                   wait a second
const text = await vfs.readText(path);                        (12)
</PRE>
<CODE>await</CODE> waits for something that takes time. It can be used at the top level of a script and
inside functions marked <CODE>async</CODE>; anywhere else it is an error.<P>

<A NAME=S14></A>
<H2>JSON</H2>
<PRE>
JSON.stringify({ a: 1, b: [2, 3] })   '{"a":1,"b":[2,3]}'    (12)
JSON.parse('{"a":1}')                 { a: 1 }
</PRE>

<A NAME=S15></A>
<H2>Dates</H2>
<PRE>
const now = new Date();                                        (9)
now.getHours()   now.getMinutes()   now.getSeconds()
now.getDate()    now.getMonth()   (0 = January)
now.getFullYear()   now.getDay()  (0 = Sunday)
Date.now()          milliseconds since 1 January 1970
</PRE>

<A NAME=S16></A>
<H2>Errors: try ... catch</H2>
<PRE>
try {                                                         (12)
  const data = JSON.parse(text);
} catch (e) {
  print('That file is damaged: ' + e.message);
}
throw new Error('Something went wrong');   make an error of your own
</PRE>
If anything in the <CODE>try</CODE> part goes wrong, the program carries on in the <CODE>catch</CODE> part
instead of stopping with an error box; <CODE>e.message</CODE> is the error's message.<P>

<A NAME=S17></A>
<H2>Going further</H2>
The book teaches the part of JavaScript you need to write complete programs, but the language has more.
These are worth looking up next, in MDN Web Docs (Mozilla's reference on the web) or any modern JavaScript
book:<P>
<DL>
<DT>Classes
<DD><CODE>class Snake { constructor() { ... } move() { ... } }</CODE> describes a kind of object and the
functions (<I>methods</I>) that belong to it, and <CODE>new Snake()</CODE> makes one.<P>
<DT>Destructuring
<DD><CODE>const { x, y } = cell;</CODE> takes properties out of an object into variables in one go;
<CODE>const [first, second] = list;</CODE> does the same for an array.<P>
<DT>Spread
<DD><CODE>[...a, ...b]</CODE> joins arrays; <CODE>{ ...p, score: 0 }</CODE> copies an object, changing one
property.<P>
<DT>Map and Set
<DD><CODE>new Map()</CODE> is a lookup table whose keys can be any value; <CODE>new Set()</CODE> is a
collection that holds each value once.<P>
<DT>Promises
<DD>What <CODE>await</CODE> actually waits for. Useful when you write your own functions that take time.<P>
</DL>

